WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:06.040
Angelina Jolie is back for Disney's Maleficent
sequel that continues to rewrite the story

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.370
of Sleeping Beauty's iconic villain.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:12.430
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm revealing and explaining twelve

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:16.920
incredible easter eggs and details you might
have missed in Maleficent: Mistress of Evil.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.810
There will be spoilers, so take care if you
haven't seen the film yet.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:25.439
At the beginning of the new movie, we find
Aurora surrounded by her fairy subjects, conducting

00:00:25.439 --> 00:00:27.409
business as Queen of the Moors.

00:00:27.409 --> 00:00:32.099
The three pixies though have devised a secret
plan to lure her to a surprise meeting with

00:00:32.099 --> 00:00:33.260
Prince Phillip.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:37.719
Pinto steals Aurora's crown and with the help
of the other magical creatures, she leads

00:00:37.719 --> 00:00:41.649
her through the forest to a spot where Phillip
is waiting to propose to Aurora.

00:00:41.649 --> 00:00:45.590
The moment is a fun twist of sorts on the
Once Upon A Dream scene from Sleeping Beauty

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:50.519
where the princess's animal friends secretly
stole Phillip's clothes and led him to her.

00:00:50.519 --> 00:00:54.320
There's also a little nod to Aurora and Phillip's
dance in Once Upon A Dream, when in the new

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.480
movie Phillip and Aurora embrace and spin
around after she accepts his proposal.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:03.699
There's another homage to a memorable moment
from Disney's classic animation during Aurora's

00:01:03.699 --> 00:01:07.220
wedding ceremony when the two pixies Knotgrass
and Thistlewit [aka the Red and Green fairies]

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.920
use magic to change the colour of Aurora's
wedding dress and argue over whether pink

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.640
or green looks better.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:16.830
This is a cute call-back to when the Red and
Blue Fairies, Flora and Merryweather, argued

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.540
over the colour of Aurora's sixteenth birthday
dress

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:21.880
"Make it pink."

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.210
"Make it blue!"

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:25.210
"Pink!"

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:29.070
In Mistress of Evil, their argument over the
dress comes to an end when the dress turns

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:33.380
blue and the two pixies turn to the blue flower
next to them, which is their fallen friend

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:36.940
Flittle, and they both say the colour is perfect.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:41.610
Many fans were disappointed that in her first
live-action movie Maleficent didn't transform

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:46.030
into a dragon as she had done in Disney's
original animated Sleeping Beauty, but instead

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:48.700
turned Diaval into a dragon instead.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:52.780
The filmmakers seem to have taken note of
that for the sequel by creating a scene where

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:58.070
Maleficent rises from the ashes as an enormous
dark phoenix, in a moment that recalls her

00:01:58.070 --> 00:01:59.950
animated dragon transformation.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:04.100
"Now shall you deal with me, O Prince.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:10.789
And all the powers of hell!"

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:15.070
Just before that moment, Maleficent appeared
to die when she was hit by a poisoned bolt

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:19.320
from Ingrith's crossbow which turned her to
dust a la Infinity War.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:23.930
But when Aurora's tears of sorrow fell on
Maleficent's ashes, it sparked the resurrection

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:28.380
of her surrogate mother who as the last of
the Phoenix's descendants has the power of

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.370
death and rebirth.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:34.920
Aurora's tears helping to bring back her mother
figure is also a nice nod to and a reverse

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:40.130
of how Maleficent's kiss saved Aurora from
the sleeping curse in the first film.

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:43.730
There's another call-back to the first Maleficent
movie in the scene after Phillip presents

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:48.290
Aurora with a Tomb Bloom flower, and Aurora
goes off to investigate where the flower came

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:49.290
from.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:53.540
After she discovers Queen Ingrith's secret
door and stairway into the castle basement,

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:58.200
Aurora hears voices whispering to her which
lead her yet again to the cursed spinning

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.560
wheel, just like they did in the first film.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.390
"[Whispering voices] Aurora..
.. on the spindle of a spinning wheel."

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:10.270
Of course, after Aurora pricked her finger
on the spindle in Sleeping Beauty, Maleficent

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:16.319
appeared and uttered her famous line:
"You poor, simple fools.

00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:18.170
Thinking you could defeat me!

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:19.170
Me!

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.670
The mistress of all evil!"

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:26.110
That title "Mistress of all evil" ended up
as the basis of the title for this Maleficent

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:27.110
sequel.

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:31.459
But what's especially interesting is that
despite Maleficent's villainous reputation

00:03:31.459 --> 00:03:36.250
being redeemed in the first movie, over the
five years that followed, Queen Ingrith spread

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:41.030
malicious rumours about her in order to foment
mistrust and suspicion between the human and

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:42.800
fairy populations.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.640
In this live-action universe, Disney's animated
version of the Sleeping Beauty fairy tale

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.260
is like a fake story that Ingrith made up.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:53.800
"I remember the story of an evil witch.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.620
And the princess she cursed to sleep forever.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:58.540
The story became legend.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:01.450
But this is no fairy tale."

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:06.000
And given everything else Ingrith does, in
this film she's the queen who really deserves

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.930
the title "Mistress of Evil".

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:13.860
In a perhaps surprising moment for a family-focussed
film, Queen Ingrith together with her henchwoman

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:18.000
goes as far as to orchestrate a Game of Thrones
Red Wedding-style scene.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:23.090
After the Moor-folk are led into the castle's
church for Aurora and Phillip's wedding ceremony,

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:28.050
Ingrith gives the order for her evil sidekick
to release the deadly red iron dust to kill

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:30.139
all the fairies inside.

00:04:30.139 --> 00:04:31.520
"Start the music."

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.349
It's a scene that brings to mind the notorious
Red Wedding from Game of Thrones with the

00:04:35.349 --> 00:04:39.800
queen's guards even barricading the fairy
guests inside the building to stop them from

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.780
escaping the lethal toxins.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:47.169
And during the attempted church massacre Pixie
Flittle sacrificed herself to protect the

00:04:47.169 --> 00:04:51.240
other trapped creatures when she flew into
the organ and, after being hit by a ball of

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.970
red powder, was transformed into an enormous
bloom of blue flowers that blocked the organ

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:59.690
pipes, stopping the release of any more toxic
powder.

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:03.969
The way that Flittle and many other fairies
turned into flowers or other flora whenever

00:05:03.969 --> 00:05:08.189
they were hit by the red iron powder reminded
me of the scene in Sleeping Beauty where the

00:05:08.189 --> 00:05:13.120
Red Fairy transformed the arrows from Maleficent's
goons into harmless flowers.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:18.699
One of the Moors' Tree Warriors also sacrificed
himself in the church by using his branches

00:05:18.699 --> 00:05:23.470
to cover and protect the other fairy creatures
from the poisonous powder exploding all around

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:24.470
them.

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:29.150
It may or may not be deliberate, but the moment
feels like a nod to Marvel's Groot and, spoiler

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:33.499
for the first Guardians of the Galaxy movie,
how he sacrificed himself by shielding the

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:38.509
other Guardians with his branches when Ronan's
ship was crash-landing to the ground.

00:05:38.509 --> 00:05:40.919
"We
are

00:05:40.919 --> 00:05:42.120
Groot."

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:47.550
By the way, Queen Ingrith seems to be inspired
by an interesting character from one of the

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.319
early Sleeping Beauty fairy tales.

00:05:49.319 --> 00:05:54.360
There are two parts to Charles Perrault's
version of the story from the late 17th century.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:59.669
In the first part, a wicked fairy is the villain,
but in the second part, the prince's mother,

00:05:59.669 --> 00:06:04.729
known as the Ogress Queen Mother, is the antagonist,
and as her name suggests she's a terribly

00:06:04.729 --> 00:06:09.419
evil character, who like Michelle Pfeiffer's
Queen Ingrith, tries but fails to kill off

00:06:09.419 --> 00:06:12.240
her daughter-in-law, sleeping beauty.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.699
There's also a few interesting fashion-related
easter eggs that the sequel's designers purposely

00:06:16.699 --> 00:06:19.039
included in Maleficent 2.

00:06:19.039 --> 00:06:22.499
Aurora's blue dress that she wears at the
beginning of the movie as Queen of the Moors

00:06:22.499 --> 00:06:26.589
and the pink dress she later wears to her
engagement dinner were both deliberately chosen

00:06:26.589 --> 00:06:31.710
by the movie's costume designer as tributes
to Aurora's pink and blue dresses from Sleeping

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:32.710
Beauty.

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:36.680
And because Elle Fanning who plays Aurora
wanted a little added something that specifically

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:41.979
paid homage to the animated film, a Sleeping
Beauty collar was added to Aurora's pink engagement

00:06:41.979 --> 00:06:43.029
dress.

00:06:43.029 --> 00:06:47.180
And Maleficent's costume at the beginning
of the movie with its "green[ish] colour [and]

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:52.030
subtle reptile pattern" also tips its hat
to her animated dragon form and its green

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:54.339
eyes and firey breath.

00:06:54.339 --> 00:06:55.439
"Don't..

00:06:55.439 --> 00:06:56.759
ruin my morning."

00:06:56.759 --> 00:07:01.409
And there's a fun double easter egg when Aurora
argues with Maleficent about her upcoming

00:07:01.409 --> 00:07:03.370
marriage to Phillip,
"What's next?

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:07.479
You'll turn him into a goat?"

00:07:07.479 --> 00:07:12.699
That line pays off later when Maleficent turns
the defeated evil queen Ingrith into a goat.

00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:14.219
"Oh, come on, that's funny."

00:07:14.219 --> 00:07:18.439
But it's also a reference to the animated
design of Maleficent's horns which were based

00:07:18.439 --> 00:07:20.229
on goat horns.

00:07:20.229 --> 00:07:24.939
So did you spot any other easter eggs or interesting
details in the Maleficent sequel?

00:07:24.939 --> 00:07:26.710
And what were your favourite moments?

